{"cid:9837243":{"key":"cid:9837243","title":"Brivaracetam","compound":{"key":"cid:9837243","cid":9837243,"qid":"Q408099","title":"Brivaracetam","iupac":"(2S)-2-[(4R)-2-oxo-4-propylpyrrolidin-1-yl]butanamide","wiki_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brivaracetam","cas":"357336-20-0","iuphar":"9041","drugbank":"DB05541","chemspider":"8012964","unii":"U863JGG2IA","kegg":null,"chebi":"133013","chembl":"CHEMBL607400","pdb_ligand":"VLX","comptox":"DTXSID00905081","echa":"100.118.642","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"N03AX23","mesh":"C482793","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:26+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"CCC[C@@H]1CC(=O)N(C1)[C@@H](CC)C(=O)N","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":2016,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":1,"control":"Schedule V (United States)","reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:41:42+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"Used as adjunctive therapy for partial-onset seizures in patients 16 years of age or older.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Drugs used to prevent SEIZURES or reduce their severity.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001382.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Milk, Human; Anticonvulsants","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1292/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Anticonvulsants","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Brivaracetam/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Brivaracetam binds SV2A with high affinity. SV2A is known to play a role in epileptogenesis through modulation of synaptic GABA release. It is thought that brivaracetam exerts its anti-epileptogenic effects through its binding to SV2A. Brivaracetam is also known to inhibit Na+ channels which may also contribute to its anti-epileptogenic action.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"The precise mechanism of brivaracetam's anti-epileptogenic activity is unknown.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Nearly 100% oral bioavailability.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"\\>95% excreted in urine with <10% of the parent compound unchanged. <1% excreted in feces.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"0.5L/kg.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"CL/F of 0.7-1.07 mL/min kg. Clearance is primarily metabolic with less than 10% of the parent drug excreted unchanged.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"<20% bound to plasma proteins.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Primarily metabolized by hydrolysis of the acetamide moeity  to form a carboxylic acid metabolite. Another metabolite is created via oxidation of the propyl side chain by CYP2C8 as well as CYP3A4, CYP2C19, and CYP2B6. Some conjugation with glucuronic acid and taurine account for a small amount of metabolism.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"7-8h.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB05541","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"LiverTox Summary","items":[{"section":"LiverTox Summary","text":"Brivaracetam is a relatively unique anticonvulsant that is typically used in combination with other antiepileptic medications for partial onset seizures. Brivaracetam has been linked to rare instances of serum aminotransferase and alkaline phosphatase elevations during treatment and is suspected of causing rare cases of clinically apparent drug induced liver disease.","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Brivaracetam/"}]},{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","items":[{"section":"Hepatotoxicity","text":"Prospective studies reported that chronic brivaracetam therapy was not accompanied by significant elevations in serum aminotransferase levels and clinically apparent liver injury was not observed. Brivaracetam has had limited general use, but has not been linked to instances of clinically apparent liver injury. Levetiracetam, an anticonvulsant with similar structure and mechanism of action, has been linked to rare instances of acute liver injury, generally arising within 1 to 20 weeks and presenting with a hepatocellular pattern of injury without immunoallergic or autoimmune features. Whether similar cases will be linked to brivaracetam is not known. Likelihood score: E (unlikely cause of clinically apparent liver injury).","source":"LiverTox","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/livertox/Brivaracetam/"}]},{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Limited information on use of brivaracetam during breastfeeding indicates that infants might have serum levels in the therapeutic range with exclusive breastfeeding, but undetectable levels with partial breastfeeding. If brivaracetam is required by the mother, it is not a reason to discontinue breastfeeding, but monitor the infant for drowsiness, agitation, adequate weight gain, and developmental milestones, especially in younger, exclusively breastfed infants and when using combinations of drugs. Measurement of infant serum levels may help rule out toxicity if there is a concern. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants Three infants, including one pair of twins, were breastfed while their mothers were taking brivaracetam. The twins were partially breastfed twice daily by a mother taking only brivaracetam and the other infant was exclusively breastfed by a mother taking brivaracetam, lacosamide and perampanel for 6 weeks, then partially breastfed. None of the infants exhibited reduced wakefulness or feeding problems. At one year of age, the mothers reported normal development. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM1292/"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{"noswallow":{"label":"No tablet or capsule to swallow","count":9,"forms":[["Oral Solution",5],["Solution for I.V injection \\Infusion",3],["Solution For I.V Infusion",1]]},"child":{"label":"Suitable for children","count":5,"forms":[["Oral Solution",5]]}},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":2016,"bbw":false,"control":"Schedule V (United States)","withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:9837243","title":"Brivaracetam","products":44,"mw":212.3,"logp":0.9,"tpsa":63.4,"hbd":1,"hba":2,"rotb":5,"rings":1,"heavy":15,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C11H20N2O2"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:5284583","title":"Levetiracetam","products":81,"mw":170.2,"logp":-0.13,"tpsa":63.4,"hbd":1,"hba":2,"rotb":3,"rings":1,"heavy":12,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C8H14N2O2","similarity":0.488},{"key":"cid:4843","title":"Piracetam","products":28,"mw":142.2,"logp":-0.91,"tpsa":63.4,"hbd":1,"hba":2,"rotb":2,"rings":1,"heavy":10,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C6H10N2O2","similarity":0.239},{"key":"cid:3000540","title":"Lincomycin","products":229,"mw":406.5,"logp":-0.86,"tpsa":122.5,"hbd":5,"hba":8,"rotb":7,"rings":2,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C18H34N2O6S","similarity":0.235},{"key":"cid:5280723","title":"Prostaglandin E1","products":3,"mw":354.5,"logp":3.48,"tpsa":94.8,"hbd":3,"hba":4,"rotb":13,"rings":1,"heavy":25,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C20H34O5","similarity":0.234},{"key":"cid:446598","title":"Clindamycin","products":53,"mw":425.0,"logp":0.39,"tpsa":102.3,"hbd":4,"hba":7,"rotb":7,"rings":2,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C18H33ClN2O5S","similarity":0.229},{"key":"cid:65028","title":"Oseltamivir","products":8,"mw":312.4,"logp":1.29,"tpsa":90.6,"hbd":2,"hba":5,"rotb":7,"rings":1,"heavy":22,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C16H28N2O4","similarity":0.226},{"key":"cid:3121","title":"Valproate","products":32,"mw":144.2,"logp":2.29,"tpsa":37.3,"hbd":1,"hba":1,"rotb":5,"rings":0,"heavy":10,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C8H16O2","similarity":0.225},{"key":"ci